WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.793
The Z-array entry z[i] stores the length of the longest common prefix between the whole string S and the suffix starting at index i.

00:00:09.793 --> 00:00:16.693
While KMP looks backward at suffixes ending at i, the Z-algorithm looks forward from index i.

00:00:16.693 --> 00:00:22.480
This direct forward view makes prefix-matching constraints effortless to read.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:30.078
Maintain the interval L to R of the match reaching furthest to the right seen so far.

00:00:30.078 --> 00:00:44.907
When index i falls inside this Z-box, the characters from i to R duplicate the prefix at offset i minus L, so initialize z[i] from z[i minus L] clipped to the box boundary.

00:00:44.907 --> 00:00:49.390
Only characters beyond R are ever compared directly.

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:57.408
Whenever a character comparison succeeds past the current right boundary R, advance R and update L to i.

00:00:57.408 --> 00:01:07.158
Because R starts at zero and never decreases, the inner while-loop executes at most N successful comparisons across the entire run.

00:01:07.158 --> 00:01:09.987
Total runtime is strictly linear O(N).

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:17.486
Just like KMP, running Z on P plus sentinel plus T flags every occurrence where z equals the length of P.

00:01:17.486 --> 00:01:25.481
Running Z on S concatenated with itself compares every cyclic shift against the original string in one linear sweep.

00:01:25.481 --> 00:01:31.821
And any index i where i plus z[i] equals N is a valid border that is both prefix and suffix.
